\title{Clean: Circuits and Their Specifications in Lean}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    An embedded [Lean](dhsorens-lean) DSL for writing zk circuits against [AIR](dhsorens-002U), Plonkish, and [R1CS](dhsorens-002S), built by zkSecurity under a verified-zkEVM grant. Circuit, specification, and proof live in the same file — an arrangement [smart contract verification](dhsorens-001J) has never had.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{How a circuit is represented, and what the proof obligation looks like — the precise form of "this circuit is sound with respect to this specification".}
        \li{Completeness as a separate obligation, and whether it is discharged or assumed.}
        \li{Lookups and VM-like table ensembles: the recent generalisation, and what it makes provable end to end.}
        \li{Witness generation by compilation to an intermediate representation, and whether the generator is verified against the constraints.}
        \li{Backends — plonky3 now, stwo and R1CS variants planned — and what a backend has to guarantee.}
        \li{How Clean's circuit specs should meet [ArkLib](dhsorens-0040)'s protocol specs. It is the load-bearing seam between the two libraries.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: [clean](https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/clean/), [Introducing clean](https://blog.zksecurity.xyz/posts/clean/).
}
